\s1 The garden at Eden
\rem /s1 Adam and Eve
\p
\v 4 What follows tells\f + \fr 2:4 \ft This little formula is sometimes known as a ‘toledoth’ (or ‘tōlədōt’, from the Hebrew word) and seems to mark a new section, but it's debated whether this part belongs at the bottom of the previous section, or at the top of the following section. We're assuming the later here.\f* \add more details about\add* the¦598 history¦598 of¦598 what God did when he created the¦599 heavens¦599 and¦600 the¦600 earth¦600:
\rem /s1 The Garden of Eden; The Man and Woman in Eden; Another Account of the Creation
\p During¦602 the time¦602 when Yahweh¦604 God¦605\f + \fr 2:4 \ft This is the first occurrence of the Hebrew letters ‘יהוה’ (YHWH) in the Bible, which we find out later (in Exodus 3, also compiled by Mosheh/Moses) is God's actual name. Although the vowels which go with those four consonants have been lost over the millennia, most scholars believe ‘Yahweh’ to be the best guess that we can make. (It's certainly NOT ‘Jehovah’, which was a historical mistake made by confusing the vowels of two different words.) Some older translations use \+sc LORD\+sc* which is NOT a translation of ‘יהוה’ (which means ‘I am’), but which represents confusion over the more recent Jewish (but non-Biblical) custom of not pronouncing God's name (and which makes one wonder what's the use of telling people your name if they're then not allowed to use it).\f* made¦603 the earth¦606 and¦607 the heavens¦607,
\v 5 there weren't yet any¦609 bushes¦611 growing¦621 on¦615 the¦615 earth¦615, and¦616 no plants¦618 had yet sprung up in the¦619 countryside¦619, because¦622 Yahweh¦625 God¦626 had¦624 not yet made¦624 it rain¦624, and¦630 nor were there¦631 any people yet to¦632 till the¦635 ground¦635.
\v 6 However, a mist came¦638 up¦638 from¦639 the¦641 ground¦641 and¦642 watered¦642 the¦647 entire¦645 surface¦647 of¦647 the¦649 land¦649.
