\p
\v 16 Then¦17030 they¦17030 moved on from¦17031 Beyt¦17031-El¦17032, and¦17033 there was¦17033 still quite a distance¦17036 to¦17039 go¦17039 to reach to \add the town of\add* Efrath. Then¦17041 Rahel¦17042 \add went into labour\add*, but¦17043 was having¦17043 great difficulty¦17043 in \add delivering the baby\add*.
\v 17 \add At one point\add* when¦17046 she was¦17046 in hard labour¦17048, the¦17051 midwife¦17051 said¦17049 to¦17050 her¦17050, “Don't¦17052 be¦17054 afraid¦17054. You've¦17060 got another son¦17061.”
\v 18 \add But Rahel\add* was dying¦17067, and¦17063 as her \add spirit\add* was¦17063 leaving her¦17065, she¦17068 named¦17068 \add the baby\add* ‘Ben'oni’ \add (which means ‘son of my sorrow’)\add*, but¦17073 \add afterwards Yacob\add* renamed him¦17076 ‘Benyamin¦17077’ \add (which means ‘son of my right hand’)\add*.
